WHY THIS PROFESSION EMERGES

Space law was built for humans. The Outer Space Treaty and Liability Convention assume decisions trace back to a human mind under national authority. That assumption is dead. Autonomous systems now make thousands of operational decisions per second without passing through any human review loop. The legal architecture has no category for an agent that does not sleep, holds no citizenship, and can trigger cascading infrastructure failure before a terrestrial court can convene.

On the Moon, the gap becomes lethal. The Liability Convention’s distinction between absolute liability on Earth and fault-based liability in space creates a jurisdictional enclave where an AI-managed reactor, processing multinational crew biometrics under one nation’s code, effectively turns the province of all mankind into a sovereign data colony. The Artemis Accords, signed by 67 nations, deliberately leave enforcement and dispute resolution to future agreements — agreements that do not exist. No Earth-side court can issue a timely injunction: the 1.3-second round-trip latency alone guarantees that any corrective order will arrive too late to prevent an irreversible resource cascade.

A new model of “limited recognition,” proposed in 2026, makes human operators legally liable for their AI envoys while preserving the autonomy those envoys need to function in deep space. But it does not answer who arbitrates when liability is distributed across three national programs, two private consortia, and a software model trained by a fourth party. The colony needs a backstop: a judicial officer who can blend telemetry forensics, multiplanetary treaty law, and game-theoretic reasoning to issue binding, machine-readable rulings at operational speed. The Algorithmic Sovereignty Judge is that backstop.

The problem is not “AI rights.” It is that autonomous systems create decision chains existing legal institutions cannot adjudicate at operational speed — and when those decisions control oxygen, water, and power, the institutional vacuum is an existential risk.

