CORE COMPETENCIES

Counterfactual Liability Analysis
Rather than demanding that an AI “explain” its internal reasoning — an increasingly unreliable demand for opaque models — the Judge determines liability by asking: Would the disputed outcome still have occurred under any admissible alternative action the AI could have taken? This causal reasoning, grounded in structural causal models and counterfactual inference, is legally more robust and technically more feasible than post-hoc explainability.

Telemetry Forensics and Evidence Authentication
Read decision logs, confidence trajectories, and sensor streams. But critically, the Judge must also verify that the evidence itself is trustworthy. All critical telemetry is secured through cryptographic evidentiary chains: signed logs, tamper-evident data structures, distributed attestation, and hardware-rooted trust. The Judge’s forensic work begins by authenticating the evidence before interpreting it.

Cross-Sovereign and Multi-Tenant Liability Mapping
Disentangle overlapping liability when an incident involves Korean hardware, French firmware, an American operator, and Japanese employment law — often across multiple AI agents. The Judge uses Shapley-value-based attribution to allocate causal and legal responsibility fairly among all contributing parties.

Algorithmic Agency Arbitration — Three-Type Taxonomy
The Judge distinguishes and adjudicates three distinct levels of AI agency:

· Type I — Operational Agency: Routine, low-risk adaptation (navigation, scheduling). Permitted without direct oversight.
· Type II — Strategic Agency: Decisions that materially affect humans (personnel assignments, resource prioritization). Requires documented oversight and post-hoc justification.
· Type III — Constitutional Agency: Attempts to alter the rules themselves — governance procedures, safety boundaries, legal permissions. Never permitted autonomously; requires explicit multi-party human authorization. Any detected drift into Type III is immediately enjoined.

Autonomous Agent Conflict Resolution
When two or more AI systems, each correctly following its encoded mandate, produce contradictory directives (e.g., “reduce O₂ reserve” vs. “maintain O₂ reserve for crew safety”), the Judge issues a priority ruling that resolves the conflict without invalidating either agent’s core function.

Game-Theoretic Strategic Disclosure Modeling
In multi-stakeholder disputes, each party may strategically withhold or distort telemetry. The Judge uses mechanism design to anticipate and neutralize such behavior, creating incentives for honest disclosure and detecting evidence manipulation.

Computational Constitutionalism
Translate legal principles into machine-verifiable constraints. For example, “No life-critical resource may be monopolized” becomes resource_share(i) < 0.35 for all actors, enforced automatically by the colony’s resource allocation system. The Judge serves as part lawyer, part constitutional compiler.

Sealed-Environment Crisis Mediation
Hold simultaneous legal authority and psychological containment in a 50-person habitat under existential pressure, preventing human conflict from destabilizing the governance system.

