SEPARATION OF POWERS

The Judge creates binding precedent — case law — but does not legislate. The statutory legal codex is the province of a separate Constitutional Assembly, which alone may alter fundamental laws. The Judge may flag gaps and propose amendments, but cannot enact them. This separation ensures that the profession does not accumulate executive, judicial, and legislative power into a single office, avoiding the historical trajectory toward benevolent legal dictatorship.

