A. LATENCY‑DIFFERENTIATED ADJUDICATION AND REVIEW STABILITY

Definition A.1 (Critical Control Window).
Let an AI‑related incident be detected at time t_0. The critical control window is the interval [t_0, t_0 + \tau], where \tau is the maximum time within which a corrective injunction must be executed to prevent irreversible resource cascade (e.g., O₂ desaturation below survivable partial pressure). Habitat engineering data place \tau in the range of tens of seconds to a few minutes.

Definition A.2 (Round‑Trip Latency).
Earth–Moon one‑way light‑time is \delta \approx 1.3 s. The earliest possible arrival of an Earth‑side judicial instruction, including minimal human decision latency, is at least t_0 + 2\delta + \Delta, where \Delta is the human response time (conservatively \ge 5 s). For any plausible \tau, 2\delta + \Delta > \tau. Hence no Earth‑side court can issue an injunction within the critical window.

Definition A.3 (Local Adjudication Protocol).
A local adjudication protocol  \mathcal{A} = (\mathcal{E}_t, \mathcal{C}, \mathcal{R})  consists of:

· \mathcal{E}_t: all telemetry, log, and sensor evidence available locally up to time t,
· \mathcal{C}: a formalised legal codex, represented as a Dung‑style argumentation framework \langle \mathcal{A}r, \leadsto \rangle where arguments are constructed from evidence and norms,
· \mathcal{R}: \mathcal{E}_t \times \mathcal{C} \to \mathbb{A}: a deterministic function mapping evidence and codex to a machine‑readable ruling (injunction, parameter bound, asset reassignment).

The protocol is self‑executing if \mathcal{R}’s output is directly ingested by the colony’s AI orchestration layer without human translation.

Theorem A.1 (Stability Under Defeasible Reasoning with Time‑Critical Evidence).
Assume the codex \mathcal{C} is expressed as a Dung argumentation framework. The local ruling r is obtained by selecting the grounded extension of the sub‑graph restricted to arguments whose supporting evidence is fully available within [t_0, t_0+\tau]. If:

1. All potential defeaters of the arguments that support r rely on evidence that cannot arrive before \tau, and
2. Earth‑side review adds only arguments whose evidence arrives after \tau,

then r remains in every complete extension of the full argument graph and is therefore stable under subsequent Earth‑side review.

Proof.
Let G_{\text{local}} be the argument graph constructed from \mathcal{E}_{t_0+\tau}. Let S_{\text{local}} be the grounded extension of G_{\text{local}}. The ruling r is the conclusion of the arguments in S_{\text{local}}. By condition (1), any defeater of any a \in S_{\text{local}} requires evidence not in \mathcal{E}_{t_0+\tau}. Earth‑side evidence arrives after \tau and thus adds no new defeaters to S_{\text{local}} that were absent locally. Hence S_{\text{local}} is admissible in the full graph G_{\infty}. Since the grounded extension is a subset of every complete extension, S_{\text{local}} remains in all complete extensions of G_{\infty}. Therefore, no justified argument in the Earth‑side review attacks the conclusion r. ∎

