B. THE JURISDICTIONAL CONFLICT MATRIX AS PRIORITISED MAX‑SAT

Definition B.1 (Norm Encoding).
Each legal source i (treaty, national law, contract) contributes a set of conditional norms N_i. A norm \phi is a rule “if P_\phi then O_\phi”, where P_\phi is a propositional formula over factual atoms and O_\phi is an obligation literal (must/must‑not/permitted). Conflicts occur when two applicable norms demand contradictory obligations in the same situation.

Definition B.2 (Norm Priority Order).
The codex provides a strict partial order \prec on norms, derived from: lex superior (higher treaty over lower), lex specialis (specific over general), lex posterior (later over earlier). We assume \prec is a directed acyclic graph.

Definition B.3 (Conflict Matrix Computation).
Given a fact situation \sigma, instantiate all norms whose precondition P_\phi is true under \sigma. Let \Phi = \{\phi_1, \ldots, \phi_m\} be the set of applicable norms, with obligations O_\phi \in \{+x, -x\}. A conflict is a pair \phi_i, \phi_j with contradictory obligations.

Proposition B.1 (Reduction to Prioritised Max‑SAT).
The problem of finding a maximal subset \Phi' \subseteq \Phi that is consistent and respects the priority order is equivalent to a Prioritised Max‑SAT instance: encode each norm as a soft clause with weight equal to its priority rank; add hard clauses forbidding simultaneous satisfaction of contradictory obligations. The maximal weighted satisfiable set yields the legally prioritised obligations.

Theorem B.1 (Termination, Consistency, and Legally Correct Output).
A greedy algorithm that processes norms in topological order of \prec and adds a norm if its obligation does not contradict any already accepted norm terminates and produces a consistent subset \Phi'. Moreover, \Phi' is maximal with respect to \prec: no superset exists that is consistent and respects the priority order. The judge’s ruling derived from \Phi' is therefore logically consistent and legally prioritised.

Proof.
Termination is immediate (finite set). Consistency holds by construction. For maximality, suppose \phi \notin \Phi'. Then at the step \phi was considered, its obligation contradicted some \phi' \in \Phi' with \phi' \prec \phi. Adding \phi would break consistency, and because \phi' has higher priority, \phi may not override it. Hence no consistent superset respecting the priority order exists. ∎

