C. COUNTERFACTUAL LIABILITY ANALYSIS VIA STRUCTURAL CAUSAL MODELS

Definition C.1 (Structural Causal Model of the Colony).
A structural causal model (SCM) M consists of endogenous variables V (resource levels, AI actions, environmental states), exogenous noise U, and a set of structural equations F. The AI agent’s admissible action set under its encoded policy is A^{\text{ad}} \subseteq \text{dom}(a), where a \in V is the agent’s action variable. For an observed action a_{\text{obs}} and outcome Y=y, the counterfactual “would Y have been different under an alternative admissible action a'?” is denoted Y_{a'}(u), computed via the three‑step procedure: abduction (update U given evidence), action (set a to a'), prediction (compute Y).

Definition C.2 (Liability Condition).
An AI agent is not liable for an incident if there exists no admissible alternative action a' \in A^{\text{ad}} such that the counterfactual outcome would have been materially safer for human life. Formally, let \text{safety}(y) be a continuous metric (e.g., minimum crew oxygen saturation over 1 hour). Then the agent is not liable iff:

\neg \exists a' \in A^{\text{ad}} : \text{safety}(Y_{a'}(u)) > \text{safety}(y_{\text{obs}}) + \delta,

where \delta > 0 is a predefined materiality threshold. If such an a' exists, the agent is liable for failing to choose it.

Theorem C.1 (Deterministic Identifiability and Computability).
Given the SCM M and complete observation of all endogenous variables, the counterfactual query Y_{a'}(u) is point‑identifiable. The liability condition can be evaluated in time polynomial in the size of the state space, assuming the number of admissible actions is bounded.

Proof.
Under full observability, abduction yields a point value for U. The counterfactual is then a deterministic function. Evaluating each a' \in A^{\text{ad}} requires one forward pass through the structural equations, which is linear in the number of variables. Hence liability determination is in O(|A^{\text{ad}}|\cdot |F|). ∎

Operational note. When full observability fails, the Judge uses bounded intervals and sets a confidence threshold, requiring the probability that a safer action existed to exceed, say, 0.95 before assigning liability.

