D. GAME‑THEORETIC STRATEGIC DISCLOSURE MECHANISM

Definition D.1 (Disclosure Game).
Let N parties each hold private evidence e_i \in \mathcal{E}_i, which may affect the liability allocation \phi. The Jurisdictional Conflict Matrix computes a baseline Shapley allocation \phi(\mathbf{e}) from the true evidence vector. Parties report \tilde{e}_i, possibly manipulated. The Judge implements a mechanism M that outputs \phi(\tilde{\mathbf{e}}) and imposes a penalty \psi_i(\tilde{e}_i, e_i) if later cryptographic verification reveals manipulation. The penalty is funded by pre‑bonded stakes.

Proposition D.1 (Truthful Equilibrium).
If (i) each party’s utility is -\phi_i (i.e., they wish to minimise their liability share), (ii) the Shapley value satisfies the balanced contributions property, and (iii) the penalty scheme uses a strictly proper scoring rule for reported evidence distributions, then truth‑telling is a Bayesian Nash equilibrium.

Proof sketch.
A strictly proper scoring rule ensures that the expected penalty is minimised (and hence the net liability cost minimised) only by reporting the true evidence distribution. Because the Shapley allocation is linear in the reported evidence’s influence on v(S), any misreport that changes v(S) alters the allocation unpredictably in a way that, combined with the scoring penalty, makes truthfulness optimal. Formal proof follows from the revelation principle for single‑parameter agents with quasilinear utilities. ∎

The Jurisdictional Conflict Matrix integrates this by flagging inconsistencies between reported evidence and the cryptographic evidentiary chains, triggering an automatic audit and penalty.

