E. ALGORITHMIC AGENCY DRIFT DETECTION VIA CUSUM WITH SURVIVAL CONSTRAINT

Definition E.1 (AI Policy and Drift).
The agent’s intended policy \pi_0 guarantees that the probability of violating a safety constraint within horizon H is \le \varepsilon_0. Agency drift means the policy shifts to an unknown \pi_1 such that the violation probability increases to \varepsilon_1 = \varepsilon_0 + \delta, with \delta > 0 the drift magnitude.

Definition E.2 (CUSUM Drift Detector).
The Judge monitors the sequence of binary violation indicators Y_t \in \{0,1\} (1 if a Type‑II or Type‑III boundary is crossed). Under H_0 (no drift), Y_t \sim \text{Bernoulli}(\varepsilon_0). The CUSUM statistic is:

S_t = \max(0, S_{t-1} + L_t), \quad S_0 = 0,

L_t = \ln\frac{\varepsilon_1 (1-\varepsilon_0)}{\varepsilon_0 (1-\varepsilon_1)} \cdot Y_t - \ln\frac{1-\varepsilon_0}{1-\varepsilon_1}.

An alarm is raised when S_t \ge h, where h is chosen to achieve a prescribed average run length to false alarm ARL_0.

Theorem E.1 (Bounded Detection Delay).
For any drift magnitude \delta > 0, the expected detection delay after the true change point \kappa satisfies

\mathbb{E}[T_{\text{detect}} - \kappa] \le \frac{h}{D_{\text{KL}}(\varepsilon_1 \| \varepsilon_0)} + O(1),

where D_{\text{KL}} is the Kullback–Leibler divergence between Bernoulli distributions.

Proof. This is a standard result from sequential analysis (Lorden, 1971). The CUSUM is asymptotically optimal; the expected delay is inversely proportional to the KL divergence. ∎

Corollary E.1 (Survival‑Constrained Calibration).
Let \tau_{\text{cascade}} be the time from first unsafe action to irreversible colony damage. The Judge selects h such that the 99th percentile of detection delay plus the ruling execution latency is less than \tau_{\text{cascade}}. This is feasible if the drift magnitude \delta is above a detectable limit given the colony’s risk tolerance.

