F. CROSS‑SOVEREIGN LIABILITY ALLOCATION VIA THE SHAPLEY VALUE

Definition F.1 (Liability Game).
Let N be the set of parties. A cooperative game (N, v) is defined where v(S) is the expected damage (or probability of incident) if the parties in S are the only ones whose components were faulty, and all others performed perfectly. The total damage is L = v(N).

Axioms (Shapley, 1953).
The Shapley value \phi_i(v) satisfies:

1. Efficiency: \sum_i \phi_i(v) = v(N).
2. Symmetry: If v(S\cup\{i\}) = v(S\cup\{j\}) for all S \subseteq N \setminus\{i,j\}, then \phi_i = \phi_j.
3. Dummy: If v(S\cup\{i\}) = v(S) for all S, then \phi_i = 0.
4. Additivity: \phi_i(v+w) = \phi_i(v) + \phi_i(w).

Theorem F.1 (Existence, Uniqueness, and Fair Allocation).
The Shapley value is the unique allocation satisfying Axioms 1–4 and is given by:

\phi_i(v) = \sum_{S \subseteq N \setminus \{i\}} \frac{|S|! (|N|-|S|-1)!}{|N|!} \left[ v(S \cup \{i\}) - v(S) \right].

Proof. Classic (Shapley, 1953). The formula computes each party’s average marginal contribution across all orders of assembly. ∎

Application. The Judge constructs v from fault‑tree analysis and the counterfactual SCM. The resulting \phi_i are embedded in the machine‑readable ruling as exact liability shares, enabling automatic compensation from bonded accounts.

