G. COLONY SURVIVAL DOMINANCE PRINCIPLE — ORIGINAL THEOREM

Definition G.1 (Existential Risk of a Ruling).
Let R(r) denote the probability of colony‑wide catastrophic failure (loss of >50% population or habitat unpressurisation) within a 72‑hour horizon after issuing ruling r, as estimated by the Jurisprudence Simulation Engine (digital twin).

Axiom G.1 (Survival Floor).
A ruling r is admissible only if R(r) < \epsilon, where \epsilon is a constitutionally fixed existential risk ceiling (e.g., 10^{-5} per day).

Theorem G.1 (Survival‑Constrained Optimal Ruling).
Let \mathcal{R} be the finite set of candidate rulings obtained from the maximal consistent norm subset (Theorem B.1) and the counterfactual liability logic. If the colony’s pre‑ruling baseline risk R_0 < \epsilon, then there exists at least one admissible ruling. Moreover, among all admissible rulings, one that maximises a weighted objective U(r) = \alpha \cdot \text{legal\_consistency}(r) + \beta \cdot \text{resource\_stability}(r) can be found by solving a constrained optimisation problem over a finite domain.

Proof.
Since R_0 < \epsilon, the “null ruling” r_0 (no intervention, leaving current settings) is admissible, so the admissible set is nonempty. The objective function U(r) is well‑defined for each r. A simple linear scan filters out rulings with R(r) \ge \epsilon and evaluates U(r) on the remainder, returning the maximiser. The finite set guarantees termination. ∎

Significance. This theorem establishes a uniquely extraterrestrial legal doctrine: legal correctness is subordinate to colony survival. No Earth‑side court has ever had to embed a probabilistic survival constraint into the definition of an admissible judgement. It is the foundational principle of multiplanetary jurisprudence.

