I. CONSISTENCY OF THE PRECEDENT SEED BANK AS A CAUSALLY CONSISTENT CRDT

Definition I.1 (Ruling DAG).
Each ruling r is a node with: unique content hash h(r), timestamp, and a set of parent hashes \text{parents}(r) (the prior rulings it directly relies on). The set of all rulings forms a directed acyclic graph G = (V, E).

Replication Protocol.
Replicas maintain a version vector and the DAG. When a new ruling is generated locally, it is broadcast. On receiving a remote ruling, if new, the replica inserts it and sets its version vector to the element‑wise maximum of the local and remote vectors.

Theorem I.1 (Strong Eventual Consistency).
The Precedent Seed Bank, implemented as an add‑only causal DAG with version vectors, achieves strong eventual consistency: any two replicas that have received the same set of rulings have identical DAGs, and all replicas eventually converge if communication is eventually available.

Proof.
The structure is an add‑only monotonic DAG (no deletions). Version vectors properly capture the causal partial order. The merge operation (taking the union of all nodes and edges, and element‑wise max of version vectors) is a least upper bound in the semilattice of version vectors, guaranteeing commutativity and idempotency. Standard CRDT theory ensures convergence. ∎

Corollary I.1 (Global Precedent Integrity).
Earth‑side courts, after synchronisation, can reconstruct the exact causal history of every lunar ruling and need never overturn a ruling due to inconsistent ordering. The legal evolution is mathematically sound.

